# Root & Rise — environment variables
#
# Everything here is optional. The client falls back to the project the app
# ships against, so a checkout builds and runs without a .env file; set the
# VITE_ pair to point a build at a different Supabase project.
#
# The publishable key is public by design — it identifies the project in the
# browser and carries no privileges of its own. Row-level security on the
# project is what protects the data behind it.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Server-side, read by api/market on the host rather than compiled into the
# browser bundle. One token serves every reader. With it the market feed is
# OANDA's real-time CFD pricing — spot gold included; without it the public
# chart feed answers, delayed and with gold priced off the COMEX future. A
# practice-account token is free: register at oanda.com and mint one under
# Manage API Access.
#
# OANDA_ENV names which of the two API hosts the token belongs to, live or
# practice. It is a hint rather than a setting: a token is good against exactly
# one host and refused by the other, so a wrong or missing value costs one
# refused request and the feed then holds whichever host answered. Naming it
# right saves that request.
OANDA_API_TOKEN=
OANDA_ENV=

# Server-side, read by api/sweep. The sweep is the only author of the published
# record, so it writes under the service role rather than the publishable key -
# which is why this one is a secret and the VITE_ pair above is not. Set
# SUPABASE_URL alongside it when the host should sweep a different project than
# the browser bundle reads.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# What the scheduler proves it is with. One sweep costs a feed read of every
# swept instrument at every timeframe, so the endpoint answers nothing without
# it. Vercel sends it as a bearer token on its own cron invocations.
CRON_SECRET=

# Where the sweep reaches the market function. It defaults to the site's own
# address, deliberately rather than to the hostname the scheduler's request
# arrived on: a deployment answers on several hostnames and the generated ones
# sit behind access protection, which turns every feed read into a sign-in page
# and every sweep into a pass that quietly reads nothing. Set this only to point
# a sweep somewhere other than production on purpose.
SWEEP_ORIGIN=

# Where a newly published call is announced, so a setup that prints while the
# reader is driving still reaches them. The browser's own notifications only
# fire while a tab is open, which is the one case they are not needed for. Any
# receiver that accepts a JSON post works - a Discord webhook URL reads the
# `content` field, most others read `text`, and both are sent. Leave it unset
# and the sweep posts to no webhook.
SIGNAL_WEBHOOK_URL=

# How a call reaches a phone. The studio's notifications endpoint holds the
# sending domain, the recipient list and the brand the message is drawn in, so
# this side holds a bearer secret and nothing else - no addresses, no mail
# credentials, nothing that would have to be rotated here.
#
# NOTIFY_SECRET is the whole switch. Without it the sweep sends no mail and
# says so in its own response rather than reporting a delivery it never
# attempted. The other two have working defaults and exist to point a
# deployment at a different endpoint or to send as a different project.
NOTIFY_SECRET=
NOTIFY_URL=
NOTIFY_PROJECT=
